I want to learn more about 3D graphics procedural terrain generation, so...

let's take a look at Marching Cubes!

![top down view of marching cubes terrain](/img/mc_header.png)

## Marching Cubes

Marching cubes is a relatively simple mesh construction algorithm first developed in [1987](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~jrs/meshpapers/LorensenCline.pdf)
to visualize medical scans, but it can be applied to any kind of 3D scalar field.

The data is sampled through a grid of cubical voxels, once for each voxel corner.
Voxel size is arbitrary, but it does determine the fidelity of the final mesh.
The sampled values are compared against the surface threshold; points less than the threshold
are underneath the mesh surface[^surface-convention]. All other points are above it. The core
of the algorithm is constructing triangles within the voxel that separate corners
on different sides of the threshold.

[^surface-convention]: This is arbitrary, you can interpret it either way.
The key idea is the isosurface cuts through edges that cross the surface isolevel


Let's look at an example. Given the following vertex and edge ids...

![wireframe cube with labeled vertexes](/img/mc_voxel_verts.png)
![wireframe cube with labeled edges](/img/mc_voxel_edges.png)

Let's say verts `1` , `5` , and `7` have low densities and are _inside_ the mesh.
This means we should create triangles from edges `5, 0, 1`, `5, 4, 0`, and `7, 6, 11`:

![wireframe cube with extra triangles inside](/img/mc_voxel_tris.png)

How do we know that? Intuitively, we know the mesh will slice through edges
that cross the surface threshold, separating low and high densities. So we place
one triangle in front of `v7`, and a two triangle quad in front of `v1` and `v5`.

Programmatically, we know we have eight corners with two possible states each,
giving us 256 possible combinations. So we can identify each case by building
an 8-bit integer from our corners. We could go through the trouble of working
out each case by hand, but fortunately, developers in the 80s have already
done this work for us. Our example voxel generates an index of  `10100010` (162),
and looking in the table, we see three triangles: `5, 0, 1`, `5, 4, 0`, and `7, 6, 11`.

## Godot Implementation

Now that we sort of know what we're doing, let's jump into Godot!

We start with a 3D scene and add a single `MeshInstance3D` with the following _tool script_.
All we're doing is generating a single mesh, so we don't really need to start up a
new godot process each time. Using the editor is fine.

{-Plus, this way we can use the inspector and save ourselves from having to write ui code.-}

```gdscript
@tool
extends MeshInstance3D

# some tool buttons so we don't have to manually reload the scene
# or reset our mesh
@export_tool_button("Generate", "Bake")
var gen_mesh_button := regenerate_mesh

@export_tool_button("Clear", "CurveDelete")
var clear_mesh_button := clear_mesh

# this is the size of our sample grid
# our voxel grid will be sample_dim - Vector3i(1)
@export var sample_dim := Vector3i(2, 2, 2)
# how much of our density field we consider above the surface
@export var surface_level := 0.0:
@export var noise: NoiseTexture3D

var terrain_mesh := ArrayMesh.new()
var terrain_verts := PackedVector3Array()
var terrain_normals := PackedVector3Array()

func _ready() -> void:
	self.mesh = terrain_mesh

func regenerate_mesh() -> void:
	pass

func density(ws: Vector3) -> float:
	return 0.0

func clear_mesh() -> void:
	terrain_mesh.clear_surfaces()
	terrain_verts.clear()
	terrain_normals.clear()
```

Be sure to reload the scene at least once to load the tool script.

To implement `regenerate_mesh()` we need to do the following:

- generate some data to sample, we'll use a 3d noise texture
- iterate over all voxels in the space:

    - sample each corner to build our case number
    - identify which edges the triangles will touch
    - calculate the final world space positions of the triangles
    - store the new triangles

- construct a single mesh from all triangles


The simplest way to iterate over each voxel is to iterate over each possible `v0` corner.
This is easy, but incredibly slow.

```gdscript
# we'll need this for sampling voxels
var samples := PackedVector4Array()
samples.resize(8)

# iterate the naive way
for y in sample_dim.y - 1:
	for x in sample_dim.x - 1:
		for z in sample_dim.z - 1:
```

Now, we sample each corner using some density function.

This density function is where all the cool terrain feature generation happens,
but for now let's hard code it to output our example voxel from earlier.

```gdscript
var v0 := Vector3(x, y, z)
var case_idx := 0
for i in 8:
	var point := v0 + offset_from_v0(i)
	var d := density(point)
	if d < surface_level:
		case_idx |= 1 << i
	# store the voxel points and their densities for the next step
	samples[i] = Vector4(point.x, point.y, point.z, d)



func density(world: Vector3) -> float:
	# v1
	if world.is_equal_approx(Vector3(1, 0, 0)): return -1.
	# v5
	elif world.is_equal_approx(Vector3(1, 1, 0)): return -1.
	# v7
	elif world.is_equal_approx(Vector3(0, 1, 1)): return -1.
	return 1.0

func offset_from_v0(id: int) -> Vector3:
	match id:
		0: return Vector3.ZERO
		1: return Vector3(1, 0, 0)
		2: return Vector3(1, 0, 1)
		3: return Vector3(0, 0, 1)
		4: return Vector3(0, 1, 0)
		5: return Vector3(1, 1, 0)
		6: return Vector3(1, 1, 1)
		7: return Vector3(0, 1, 1)
	return Vector3.ZERO
```

Now we can grab the triangle configuration from the lookup table and interpolate
along the edges to find where they will sit in world space. This interpolation
determines how smooth our terrain will be.

Here `LUT` is a named class with our lookup tables.
Checkout the [source](https://github.com/deparr/marching-cubes) or this [write up](https://paulbourke.net/geometry/polygonise/) for details.

```gdscript
# voxel is either entirely inside or outside the mesh
if case_idx == 0 or case_idx == 255:
    continue

# in order to be const (and run in editor) this array has to be typed
# as Array[Array], instead of Array[PackedInt32Array], kinda annoying
var tri_edges := LUT.tris[case_idx]
for i in tri_data.size() / 3:
	var t := i * 3;
	# these are the voxel edges our triangle touches
	var e0: int = tri_data[t]
	var e1: int = tri_data[t + 1]
	var e2: int = tri_data[t + 2]

	# these tables index from voxel edge id to it's endpoint vertex ids
	# eg. edge 0 gives us 0 and 1 for a and b respectively
	var e0_a := LUT.vertex_id_from_edge_a[e0]
	var e0_b := LUT.vertex_id_from_edge_b[e0]

	var e1_a := LUT.vertex_id_from_edge_a[e1]
	var e1_b := LUT.vertex_id_from_edge_b[e1]

	var e2_a := LUT.vertex_id_from_edge_a[e2]
	var e2_b := LUT.vertex_id_from_edge_b[e2]

	# interpolate between those verts according to the surface level
	var a := interp_edge(samples[e0_a], samples[e0_b])
	var b := interp_edge(samples[e1_a], samples[e1_b])
	var c := interp_edge(samples[e2_a], samples[e2_b])
	var normal := (c - a).cross(b - a).normalized()

	# store the final points
	terrain_verts.push_back(a)
	terrain_verts.push_back(b)
	terrain_verts.push_back(c)
	terrain_normals.push_back(normal)
	terrain_normals.push_back(normal)
	terrain_normals.push_back(normal)


# xyz is world space, w is sampled density
# returns interpolated world space pos
func interp_edge(v0: Vector4, v1: Vector4) -> Vector3:
	# always use halfway point, results in chunky, angled terrain
	return (Vector3(v0.x,v0.y,v0.z) + Vector3(v1.x,v1.y,v1.z)) * 0.5

	# linearly interpolate across the edge, results in smoother terrain
	# var t := (surface_level - v0.w) / (v1.w - v0.w)
	# var lerped = v0 + ((v1 - v0) * t)
	# return Vector3(lerped.x, lerped.y, lerped.z)
```

Once we have all of our triangles, we construct our mesh.

Godot's `ArrayMesh` class does the work for us.

```gdscript
if terrain_verts.size() > 0:
	var mesh_data = []
	mesh_data.resize[Mesh.ARRAY_MAX]
	mesh_data[Mesh.ARRAY_VERTEX] = terrain_verts
	mesh_data[Mesh.ARRAY_NORMAL] = terrain_normals
	terrain_mesh.create_surface_from_arrays(Mesh.PRIMITIVE_TRIANGLES, mesh_data)
else:
	print("None of the voxels cleared the surface level!")
```

Now if we set `sample_dim = (2, 2, 2)` and click our generate button,
a mesh identical to our example voxel from earlier should appear.

Nice!

## Making Terrain

Okay, we can make a single voxel, how do we turn this into terrain?

First thing we can do is scale up our sample space.
Reminder that we iterate over this in nested, serial loops; our generator's performance drops insanely quick:

- `16x16x16` is instant but isn't interesting
- `32x32x32` takes \~0.5s, also isn't interesting
- `64x32x64` takes \~2s, and starts to look like terrain
- `128x32x128` takes \~10s, and is the sweet spot for our bad implementation
- `512x32x512` takes \~120s, but is pretty cool
- `1024x32x1024` takes long enough that it prompted me to track time
  stats and I didn't do it again

Next thing we need is actual data to sample. A standard `NoiseTexture3D` works fine here.
I had good results using a small, 16x16x16 Value noise texture with medium frequency (\~0.2) and very low fractal gain (\~0.06).

Let's update our density function to sample the noise:

```gdscript
func density(world: Vector3) -> float:
	# start with negative y coord to mostly guarantee we have a ground plane
	var d := -world.y

	# remap coords so y samples depth instead
	# optionally we can always set y 0, which  *usually* ensures the
	# terrain has no overhanging features
	var noisec = Vector3(world.x, world.z, world.y)

	# low frequency at high amplitudes creates large structures like mountains and canyons
	# we also rescale the noise from [0, 1] to [-1, 1] so it both adds
	# to and subtracts from the surface height
	d += (1. - 2. * noise.noise.get_noise_3dv(noisec * 0.08927)) * 8
	d += (1. - 2. * noise.noise.get_noise_3dv(noisec * 0.4958)) * 4
	d += (1. - 2. * noise.noise.get_noise_3dv(noisec * 0.234)) * 2

	# high frequency at low amplitudes creates small details, but at the
	# scales our cpu marcher can handle they don't look very good
	# d += (1. - 2. * noise.noise.get_noise_3d(world.x * 8, world.z, world.y / 2)) * .2
	# d += (1. - 2. * noise.noise.get_noise_3d(world.x * 2, world.z * 4, world.y)) * .1

	return d
```

With that we start to get something that resembles terrain.


![128x32x128 chunk of angular, solid colored terrain](/img/mc_angular_no_ramp.png)

Looking good!

{.callout}
> Though I should note that you need far more noise layers to get realistic
> terrain, at least 7 layers spanning various frequencies and amplitudes.

Right now our triangle vertices are always being interpolated to the midpoint
of voxel edges. Swapping that for a linear interpolation will smooth out
those harsh angles.

Using a custom shader we can also do height based coloring: 

![128x32x128 chunk of smooth naturally colored mountains](/img/mc_smooth_ramp.png)

## Optimizing

Obviously our current generator is ungodly slow. It runs on the cpu, processing
a single voxel at a time. We could cap our chunk size to something small and
spin up more cpu threads, but that's like counting out loud by 10 instead of 1 and
expecting to reach a million any faster.

To make this meaningfully faster we need use the gpu to process thousands of voxels in parallel.

{.callout}
> Up until now I've been avoiding looking at others' implementations,
> but I'm not super familiar with compute shaders yet.
> So I'm leaving the compute shader code out of the post because a) I
> didn't a good chunk of it, b) my understanding of it is shaky at best,
> and c) it's pretty long.

Running this as a compute shader requires a lot more boilerplate code
to handle data transfer to and from the gpu, though the actual shader looks
similar to our serial gdscript code.

{.aside .note}
> Small aside, working with compute shaders really makes me wish gdscript
> had a struct type with well defined layout semantics. Or at least something
> similar to python's [struct tools](https://docs.python.org/3/library/struct.html).
> C# has these things, but is missing some tool script features; what can you do


I'm still not very good at tweaking the noise layers to get nice looking terrain,
but at least I can iterate quickly with the newfound speed of the compute shader.

![desert valley terrain](/img/mc_comp_valley.png)

With a little bit of lighting and fog, it makes nice caves too.

![dark foggy cave](/img/mc_comp_cave.png)

## Next Steps

I like the small terrains I was able to make but there's still many improvements to make:

- mesh topology, right now my triangles don't share vertices at all
- learning how to properly layer noise to make natural terrain
- exploring how to create specific terrain features from only math; caves, plains, etc
- implementing a chunk system to support an infinite world
- proper texturing and coloring, not just height based
- better understanding of gpu memory instead of allocating a "seems good
  enough" amount of triangles and hoping

Oh well, there's always more to learn!

Thanks for reading!

Source for both implementations is on [github](https://github.com/deparr/marching-cubes).


